---
/**
 * The fleet and the four opponents, drawn once.
 *
 * ⚠️ **A file of its own because two components need the same drawings**: the
 * game itself, and the thumbnail on the games index, which is a real board at a
 * card's size rather than a picture of one. Copying a hundred lines of hull
 * into the second of them is exactly how a card ends up advertising a game that
 * no longer looks like that.
 *
 * ⚠️ **The ids are global, so this must appear once per document.** It never
 * appears twice as things stand - the board is only on the game page and the
 * thumbnail only on the index - and if that ever changes, this is the note that
 * says why the second one is a duplicate id rather than a second sprite sheet.
 */
---

{/*
  The fleet: five vessels, and five silhouettes of them.

  ⚠️ **Two symbols per ship, and the pair is the whole trick.** A hull is drawn
  eight times over, stacked up the Z axis, to give it a side you can see on a
  tilted board - and only the top copy is ever looked at. So `bs-ship-N` carries
  the turrets, the funnels, the flight deck and the dive planes, and
  `bs-hull-N` is the outline alone, which is what the seven copies underneath
  use. Detail is resolved once per ship instead of eight times.

  ⚠️ **The two outlines have to stay the same shape.** The detailed symbol
  starts with the same path the silhouette is, because the silhouette is what
  the flank underneath is extruded from - change one without the other and the
  side of the ship stops lining up with the deck on top of it.

  ⚠️ **Every vessel is a plan view, bow to the right, lit from the top.** The
  rounding is bands rather than gradients: a `<linearGradient>` inside a symbol
  is not reliably resolved from inside a `<use>` shadow tree, while a flat fill
  mixed off `--bs-hull` always is. Five bands - a lit sheer, the deck, the
  flank, the shaded flank and the boot topping at the waterline - read as a
  round hull and cost nothing to resolve.

  ⚠️ **Detail is layered by contrast, not by count.** Everything that has to
  survive a ship two centimetres long - the silhouette, the deck, the turrets,
  the island, the sail - carries a full step of tone. Everything that is there
  for the close look - plating seams, panel lines, guardrails, hatch covers -
  is a hairline a few percent off its ground, so it enriches the hull at size
  and fades out rather than turning into noise on the board.

  ⚠️ **Colour goes in through `style`, never through a `fill` attribute.** These
  are `<use>`d, and a document stylesheet cannot reach inside that shadow tree -
  inherited custom properties are the only thing that gets in. That is also what
  makes a wreck one rule: `.bs-hull.is-sunk` redefines the tokens and every
  shape in here follows, down to the red at the waterline.

  A vertical ship is the same drawing turned a quarter by the stylesheet, which
  is why nothing in here has a top or a bottom that only works one way up.
*/}
<svg class="bs-sprites" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
  <defs>
    {/* ---- The silhouettes, for the flank ------------------------------- */}
    <symbol id="bs-hull-0" viewBox="0 0 500 100">
      <path
        d="M8 24 L112 10 L440 12 L494 50 L440 88 L112 90 L8 76 Z"
        style="fill:var(--bs-hull)"></path>
    </symbol>

    <symbol id="bs-hull-1" viewBox="0 0 400 100">
      <path
        d="M10 32 L30 16 L352 14 L396 50 L352 86 L30 84 L10 68 Z"
        style="fill:var(--bs-hull)"></path>
    </symbol>

    <symbol id="bs-hull-2" viewBox="0 0 300 100">
      <path
        d="M10 33 L28 18 L252 16 L296 50 L252 84 L28 82 L10 67 Z"
        style="fill:var(--bs-hull)"></path>
    </symbol>

    <symbol id="bs-hull-3" viewBox="0 0 300 100">
      <path
        d="M6 50 L30 33 C46 29 70 27 96 26 L206 25 C250 27 282 36 294 50 C282 64 250 73 206 75 L96 74 C70 73 46 71 30 67 Z"
        style="fill:var(--bs-hull)"></path>
    </symbol>

    <symbol id="bs-hull-4" viewBox="0 0 200 100">
      <path
        d="M10 33 L26 18 L152 16 L196 50 L152 84 L26 82 L10 67 Z"
        style="fill:var(--bs-hull)"></path>
    </symbol>

    {/*
      Carrier. A flight deck wider than the hull it sits on, an island pushed
      to one side, an angled deck across the port quarter and aircraft parked
      on it. Those four together are what make this readable as a carrier at
      two centimetres long, and none of them is a detail that can be dropped.

      Everything else - the deck-edge lifts hanging off both sides, the two bow
      catapult tracks, the arrestor wires across the landing area, the blast
      deflectors behind the cats, the sponsons under the deck edge - is what
      the deck is *for*, and it is what stops a carrier reading as a grey
      lozenge with arrows on it once the board is any bigger than a thumbnail.
    */}
    <symbol id="bs-ship-0" viewBox="0 0 500 100">
      <path
        d="M8 58 L8 78 L112 92 L442 90 L498 52 L492 46 L440 86 L114 88 L14 72 Z"
        style="fill:var(--bs-boot)"></path>

      {/* The hull under the deck, and the sponsons hung off its sides. */}
      <path d="M26 30 L52 18 L406 16 L478 50 L406 84 L52 82 Z" style="fill:var(--bs-hull)"></path>
      <path
        d="M26 50 L52 82 L406 84 L478 50 Z"
        style="fill:color-mix(in oklab, var(--bs-hull) 74%, #000)"></path>
